    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Guide dogs are the “eyes” of blind people. But how can they be so helpful?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The answer to this question is simple. Guide dogs practise to be helpful, and practice makes perfect."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "As part of their training, guide dogs practise getting to and around the most common places their owners need to go. These places may include the shops near their home."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "But before this, guide dogs must receive training in many other areas. This is because guiding a person safely is more important than knowing where to go."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Suppose a tree branch is lying on the path in front of a blind person and his guide dog. Since the person cannot see the branch, it’s up to the dog to let him know it is there. If the branch is small, the dog may help guide its owner around it. If it is large, the dog will block the person so that he knows there is something in the way. The person will then work with his dog to find a way past the branch."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Many people think a guide dog tells its owner when it is safe to cross a road. This is not true. Instead, the dog usually blocks its owner when they have reached the end of a path. It is then up to the person to listen to their surroundings and decide if it is safe to cross the road. A blind person and his dog always work as a team."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
